content = r"""# Patroner — Architecture Baseline & Development Blueprint

**Version:** 0.1  
**Status:** Proposed engineering baseline  
**Date:** 2026-10-31

## 1. Purpose and governing architecture

Patroner is an AI-powered personalized fashion engineering platform. It converts design intent, reference assets, approved body measurements, garment knowledge, and material constraints into validated pattern and production artifacts.

The target is a **hybrid Python + Next.js system**:
- **Python** is authoritative for AI-assisted interpretation, fashion knowledge, deterministic drafting, geometry, grading, simulation, validation, orchestration, provenance, and artifact production.
- **Next.js** owns the user-facing workspace, project interaction, review, visualization, collaboration, and export flows.
- The systems communicate through explicit, typed, versioned contracts. The frontend must not duplicate authoritative garment calculations.
- Start modular. Split into independently deployed services only when scale, reliability, security, isolation, or team ownership demonstrates a concrete need.
- Database, queue, object storage, graph technology, hosting, and solver choices remain open until requirements and experiments justify them.

This is a proposed baseline, not a claim that these components already exist in the repository.

## 2. Authority and engineering rules

1. Approved user measurements are authoritative. Image-derived or AI-inferred estimates remain suggestions until explicitly reviewed and approved.
2. Deterministic geometry and explicit constraints govern pattern coordinates, pieces, seams, allowances, notches, grainlines, and grading.
3. AI may interpret references, propose attributes, and recommend parameters; it cannot silently override approved inputs or bypass validation.
4. Every draft, report, simulation, and export records immutable source revisions and versioned engine/configuration identities.
5. Visual plausibility is not production readiness. Relevant geometric, construction, material, and manufacturing checks must pass.
6. Human approval is an explicit state transition.
7. Failures preserve provenance and permit safe retry; partial artifacts must not be published as complete.

## 3. Logical architecture

### Next.js product layer
Responsibilities: authentication-aware application shell; tenant/project workspace and revision history; design brief/reference capture; measurement entry and approval; generation submission and progress; pattern visualization; validation findings; approval workflows; production-sheet review, export, and collaboration.

Non-responsibilities: authoritative drafting calculations, grading/seam geometry, model-provider secret handling, or persistence internals owned by the backend.

### Python domain and computational layer
Organize as logical modules initially:
1. Identity/access adapter — validates principal and tenant context.
2. Project/revision domain — immutable snapshots, dependencies, expected-head checks, audit lineage.
3. Fashion knowledge model — garment taxonomy, construction semantics, terminology, versioned facts.
4. Intent interpretation — structured candidate attributes, confidence, unresolved questions.
5. Measurement domain — definitions, units, provenance, validation, approval.
6. Recipe/constraint compiler — approved intent into inspectable drafting plan.
7. Deterministic pattern engine — curves, darts, panels, seam topology, allowances, notches, grainlines.
8. Grading engine — explicit size rules and measurement-derived transformations.
9. Material/behavior model — bounded simulations and documented assumptions.
10. Workflow orchestration — durable operation state, retries, cancellation, dependencies.
11. Validation/evaluation — machine-readable checks, severity, evidence, release gates.
12. Artifact production — versioned patterns, production sheets, instructions, export packages.
13. Quality feedback — production outcomes and defect/correction records with governed learning.

## 4. Canonical domain model

Conceptual entities (not a database mandate):
- Tenant, User, Membership, AccessGrant
- FashionProject and immutable ProjectRevision
- ReferenceAsset, DesignBrief
- BodyProfile and MeasurementSet
- Recipe / RecipeVersion
- PatternDraft / PatternPiece / SeamRelation
- MaterialSpecification, EnginePlan
- Operation / Job, ValidationReport, SimulationRun
- Approval, Artifact
- ProductionEvent, Defect, CorrectiveAction, AuditEvent

Use stable IDs and explicit versions. Internal dimensional calculations use centimetres unless a type declares otherwise; serialize units explicitly. Support standard XS, S, M, L, XL, XXL as a documented starting chart and custom measurements. Document chart source, measurement definitions, grading rules, and limitations. A size label is not a fit guarantee.

## 5. Revision, provenance, and dependency integrity

A revision records parent revision, expected project-head version, approved measurement revision, brief and reference checksums, recipe/rules, engine/model/configuration versions, material assumptions, actor, timestamp, and approvals.

Revisions are immutable. Advance project head with an expected-head comparison to prevent lost updates. Derived drafts and artifacts record exact source fingerprints. Upstream changes mark affected results stale unless compatibility is proven.

Long-running work uses idempotency keys and durable operation records. Retries must not duplicate approvals, exports, or production events. Artifact publishing is staged: generate, validate, then expose as complete.

## 6. Typed contracts and API boundary

Define language-neutral schemas for requests, responses, events, and persisted interchange. Generate or validate TypeScript and Python types from a shared schema source where practical. Contracts specify schema/version, required fields, null semantics, units/tolerances, provenance/confidence, validation, compatibility, and error/retry semantics.

Illustrative routes (not final commitments):
- `POST /api/v1/projects`; `GET /api/v1/projects/{project_id}`
- `POST /api/v1/projects/{project_id}/revisions`
- `POST /api/v1/projects/{project_id}/interpretations`
- `POST /api/v1/projects/{project_id}/drafts`
- `GET /api/v1/operations/{operation_id}`; `POST /api/v1/operations/{operation_id}/cancel`
- `GET /api/v1/artifacts/{artifact_id}`; `POST /api/v1/approvals`

Use pagination, correlation IDs, idempotency keys, and structured errors (RFC 9457 Problem Details is a candidate). Tenant identity comes from validated server-side authorization context, never an untrusted client field.

## 7. Asynchronous operation lifecycle

Lifecycle: `accepted → queued → running → awaiting_review → succeeded`. Other states: `failed`, `cancellation_requested`, `cancelled`, `expired`. Define legal transitions centrally and store structured stage events.

Workers revalidate authorization and revision eligibility when executing. Record attempt, retry reason, timestamps, resource limits, and terminal error classification. Retry transient infrastructure failures, not invalid inputs, failed constraints, revoked access, or review rejection.

## 8. Core workflows

### Reference-to-pattern
1. Create project and submit reference plus brief.
2. Record checksum, tenant scope, and source metadata.
3. Interpret candidate attributes, construction hypotheses, confidence, and open questions.
4. User reviews/approves or edits interpretation.
5. Select approved measurements or enter custom values.
6. Compile versioned recipe/engine plan and expose assumptions.
7. Generate deterministic geometry and piece relationships.
8. Validate geometry, seams, allowances, notches, grainlines, construction.
9. User reviews findings and approves eligible revision.
10. Stage, validate, and publish artifacts.

### Measurement, revision, and recovery
The UI distinguishes standard sizes from custom values and labels estimates versus approved measurements. A photo never silently replaces a manually approved value. Changes to brief, measurement, recipe, or material assumption create a new revision and invalidate dependent outputs. Failures preserve evidence; corrected-input resubmission creates a new operation/fingerprint. Never relabel incomplete output as successful.

## 9. Validation architecture

Separate validators for:
- schema/input completeness and measurement plausibility;
- geometric validity and self-intersection;
- seam pairing and length compatibility within declared ease/tolerance;
- darts/construction constraints;
- seam allowance continuity and corner handling;
- notch placement/pairing and grainline;
- grading monotonicity and piece topology;
- material/behavior constraints;
- artifact integrity and export round-trip.

Each finding includes stable code, severity, message, affected entity/location, observed value, expected rule/range, evidence, and validator version. Define which findings block drafting, approval, or export. Calibrate thresholds; do not invent them.

## 10. Security, privacy, and tenant boundaries

Authorize every object and operation. Verify tenant scope for API, assets, artifacts, jobs, caches, search, analytics, exports, and backups. Grants are scoped and revocable; recheck at worker execution and artifact download.

Keep model credentials server-side. Minimize image/body-profile retention and document purpose, retention, deletion, and provider handling. No cross-tenant model training by default. Audit grants, approvals, exports, and sensitive changes. Threat-model uploads, prompt injection in reference content, malformed model output, and resource exhaustion.

## 11. Observability and quality engineering

Use correlation IDs across requests, operations, and artifact lineage. Measure stage latency, queue wait, retries, validation failures, export errors, and resource consumption. Avoid logging private images, body measurements, credentials, or unnecessary personal data.

Testing layers: unit; property-based geometry/grading invariants; Python/TypeScript contract; persistence/access integration; end-to-end workflows; golden pattern fixtures; load/fault injection; security and tenant isolation. Passing software tests is not proof of fit across bodies or production conditions. Validate with physical samples and documented wearer/maker feedback.

